 "capsule": "This label spans the long arc of Serbian monarchy, beginning with the state Stefan Nemanja forged as grand prince of Raška: his son Stefan the First-Crowned obtained a royal crown in 1217, and the Nemanjić kingdom peaked when Stefan Dušan was crowned emperor in 1346, before Ottoman victories on the Maritsa (1371) and at Kosovo (1389) reduced Serbia to a despotate extinguished at Smederevo in 1459. Revived by the uprisings of 1804 and 1815 as an autonomous principality, Serbia won full independence at the 1878 Congress of Berlin and was proclaimed a kingdom under Milan Obrenović in 1882; after the 1903 assassination of Alexander Obrenović the throne passed to Peter I Karađorđević, whose reign saw Serbia double its territory in the Balkan Wars of 1912-1913. The combined Austro-German-Bulgarian offensive of autumn 1915 overran the country, forcing army and government into a disastrous winter retreat across the Albanian mountains to exile on Corfu, from where the kingdom fought on through 1916.",
